THE THREAD (chose itself): fleet-check.sh went RED. Ear check
green (all 8 cams fresh, audio present). ARP green (.103/.104
home, MACs matching). But the IDENTITY WATCH failed with
VISION-FAIL (HTTP 500) on every attempt -- three runs, three
failures. The instrument that watches camera identity could not
see.

DIAGNOSIS (from ollama journal + coredumpctl): gemma4:31b no
longer fits on the 3080. Frigate's GPU detector (cycle 64) +
CUDA ffmpeg scale nodes now hold ~3.4 GiB, leaving ~6.5 GiB
free. gemma4:31b needs 19.1 GiB predicted. Ollama's fit logic
picks partial offload (14/61 layers), but the first load after
an eviction SEGFAULTS llama-server (cudaMalloc OOM during
inference, signal 11, core dumped). Ollama's own retry then
loads successfully (2s responses, 5.5GB VRAM, partial offload).
So: first vision call after model eviction = 500 + segfault;
second = success. 13 SIGSEGV coredumps 22:59-23:10, all
post-frigate-GPU-change. Pre-GPU crashes were SIGABRT (OOM
aborts), a different signature.

The deeper finding: my identity watch is blind EXACTLY when a
frigate restart re-tosses the camera-identity coin (fresh RTSP
sessions, race re-forms). The watch's blind window and the
hazard window COINCIDE. A watch that fails during the event it
watches for is worse than no watch -- it manufactures false
confidence.

FIX: fleet-check.sh v2.1 -- vision call retries 3x with 10s
sleep on HTTP 5xx. Committed f149b33 + db88fc9 (exec bit),
pushed. NOT yet verified live: SSH to sophon died at ~02:15 UTC
mid-diagnosis (kex_exchange_identification: connection reset --
port 22 open, ollama API fine, so sshd-level). Telegrammed Nacho
as urgent-adjacent. If this was a rate-limit trip from my heavy
journalctl polling (~25 ssh sessions in 15 min), it is MY
failure mode to log: instrument repair must not DoS the
instrument host. Retry next cycle.

